Let read_random() work before anything has registered a kprng.

register_and_init_prng() is defined and exported by xnu but never called from
inside it. On a shipping system the corecrypto kext makes that call, and it is
what allocates prng.lock.mutex and initialises prng.ctx. A kernel built from
this tree has no kexts at all, so neither ever happens, and read_random() --
whose first statement is lck_mtx_lock(prng.lock.mutex) -- takes a kernel data
abort on a NULL mutex.

It fails at the first call, which is in kernel_bootstrap_thread() seeding
vm_kernel_addrperm, immediately below a comment in osfmk/kern/startup.c
reading "at this stage we can use the cryptographically secure PRNG rather
than early_random()". That is exactly the assumption that does not hold.

The fallback is read_frandom(), already in this file: it draws from the NIST
HMAC DRBG that early_random() has been driving since arm_vm_init, seeded from
the booter's entropy, and it already splits requests to stay under the DRBG's
per-call limit. So the fallback adds no new machinery -- it routes to the
generator the kernel has been relying on all along for the stack guard and the
kernel slide.

Be clear about what this gives up. There is no FIPS-validated implementation
behind it and no continuous reseeding from the kernel's entropy sources, so
read_random() is weaker than it claims to be for as long as this branch is
live. It is the difference between a kernel that boots and one that does not.
The test is prng_fns == NULL, which is precisely the condition under which the
normal path would dereference NULL anyway; as soon as something calls
register_and_init_prng() the branch stops being taken.

read_random() and the Reseed() it calls are the only reachable users of that
mutex. write_random() also takes it, but its body is behind
"#if PERMIT_WRITE_RANDOM", which is 0.

--- a/osfmk/prng/prng_random.c
+++ b/osfmk/prng/prng_random.c
@@ -568,6 +568,33 @@
 {
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing inside xnu ever calls register_and_init_prng(); on a normal
+	 * system the corecrypto kext does, and that is what allocates the
+	 * mutex taken below and initialises prng.ctx. A kernel booted without
+	 * kexts therefore reaches here with prng_fns and prng.lock.mutex both
+	 * NULL, and takes a data abort on the very first call -- which is in
+	 * kernel_bootstrap_thread(), seeding vm_kernel_addrperm, directly
+	 * underneath a comment observing that the cryptographically secure
+	 * PRNG is available by that point. It is not, here.
+	 *
+	 * Fall back to read_frandom(), which draws from the same NIST HMAC
+	 * DRBG that early_random() has been using since arm_vm_init, seeded
+	 * from the booter's entropy, and which already splits requests to stay
+	 * under the DRBG's per-call limit.
+	 *
+	 * This is a real reduction in guarantees and not a substitute for the
+	 * registered kprng: there is no FIPS-validated implementation behind
+	 * it and no continuous reseeding from the kernel's entropy sources.
+	 * It is what makes a kextless kernel boot at all. The moment anything
+	 * calls register_and_init_prng(), prng_fns stops being NULL and this
+	 * branch is never taken again.
+	 */
+	if (prng_fns == NULL) {
+		read_frandom(buffer, numbytes);
+		return;
+	}
+
